  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 PM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A couple of cold fronts will move south and across \ncntl NC Fri through Sat, with moisture and instability maximized and \nmost supportive of showers/storms between the two. \n\nAn anomalously strong subtropical to lwr mid-latitude ridge may \nyield an observed 500 mb height of 600 dam at FFC or BNA this \nevening, while retrograding across the TN and lwr MS Valleys through \nSat. Nwly flow around the nern periphery of the ridge may direct at \nleast a few convectively-enhanced mid-level disturbances from the OH \nValley to the (particularly wrn) Carolinas Thu through Fri night, \nbefore a col and area of weak and variable flow becomes established \nover cntl NC this weekend.  \n\nAt the surface, a couple of closely-spaced fronts, now stretching \nand modulated by convection across the upr OH through mid MS \nValleys, will progress somewhat discontinuously swd across the OH \nValley and Middle Atlantic through early Fri. The lead front, \npressure trough, and convergence axis will progress generally sewd \nacross cntl NC through the day Fri, and through a residually-\ndry/continental pre-frontal airmass deposited by the preceding \nCanadian high and dry air ridge. Low-level moisture and instability \n--and favored region for showers/storms-- will be maximized between \nthat lead front and a trailing, backdoor segment that will meanwhile \nprogress swwd through VA and into-through cntl NC late Fri through \nearly Sat. The timing of those features and instability axis would \nfavor showers/storms over the nrn Piedmont and nrn Coastal Plain Fri \nafternoon-evening, then the Sandhills and srn-cntl Coastal Plain Fri \nnight-Sat. Weak lapse rates and shear will limit storm intensity \nthroughout the period.",
